the horror of sarah everard's final  hours prompted an outpouring of grief  but it was the fact that the man  responsible was a serving police officer  which added to the disbelief at her fate  wayne cousins pretended to be on duty  when he kidnapped the 33 year old before  raping and then murdering her earlier  this year  his case is extreme but he's not alone  in abusing a position of trust for  sexual gain  and the number of cases being reported  has soared  the abuse of position for a sexual  purpose currently makes up nearly 60  percent of investigations by the  independent office for police conduct  from 2018 to 2021 66 officers and  members of police staff faced  disciplinary proceedings as a result  42 of those in the past year  in 63 cases misconduct was proven  with six people convicted of crimes  those figures have gone up because it's  become a mandatory referral so people  have to refer it to us now but we would  hope that also it reflects a increased  awareness  where we are seeing fellow officers for  example reporting people that they  believe might be doing that and those  are being referred to us as well  it hasn't reassured former animal rights  activist jessica who was unaware her  partner of a year back in the 90s was an  undercover officer if they break the law  then surely there should be you know  absolute zero  tolerance policy  and you know it needs again it's that  it's the transparency you know far too  many police officers  that have done something wrong you know  it's all swept under the carpet you know  there's the the sort of the policy of  you know the old boys club  the organizers of the vigil held after  sarah everard's murder also want more to  be done  if we are only measuring instances of  harassment and violence we're missing  all of the warning signs of misogyny  this report uh from the iopc talks about  um  sexual offences uh where an officer  abuses their power well that's the worst  case scenario before that point that  officer has probably  done things which make women  uncomfortable when cousin's nickname was  the rapist the iopc is clear that those  who abuse their position for sexual gain  will be found out  but there's work to be done to rebuild  trust in those whose roles should be to  protect  emma birchley sky news
 

